# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reverse-path-forwarding check — a small figure that reads RIGHT TO LEFT.
#
# What this figure probes: four constructs that had no demonstrator anywhere in
# the corpus before this release. They are gathered into one small document on
# purpose — a capability needs A demonstrator, not a document of its own.
#
#   1. `flow left`   — the reverse direction. Twelve figures in this corpus use
#                      `flow right` and twelve use `flow down`; `left` and `up`
#                      had none, so half of the enum was untested by any figure.
#   2. `\"` escape   — a literal double quote INSIDE a quoted label.
#   3. `#` inside a  — a `[...]` region is verbatim (`VERBATIM-REGION-SCOPE`): the
#      bracketed        comment mark loses its meaning inside one, so an edge
#      edge label       label may carry a `#` without quoting or escaping it.
#   4. a NEGATIVE   — `at=` is canvas px and the origin is not a wall. A pin may
#      `at=` pin        sit at a negative coordinate; the engine translates the
#                       whole scene so the canvas still starts at (0,0).
#
# The subject fits the direction: an RPF check runs BACKWARDS along the path a
# packet arrived on, so a figure that reads right to left is the honest layout,
# not a stunt. That is the test for `flow left` in real use — if reversing the
# reading order does not say something about the subject, use `flow right`.
figdown 0.1 block
title "RPF check on a multicast join"

class fail "RPF failure — the packet arrived on an interface that is not the route back to the source" stroke=#b91c1c style=dashed

# The source is on the RIGHT because the check walks back TOWARDS it.
external src "from 198.51.100.7"
# `\n` (a line break) and `\\` (a literal backslash) are the other two escapes
# the grammar defines — core §11 lists exactly three, and all three are in this
# file. The interface filter is a regex, which is where a backslash occurs
# naturally in a network figure rather than as a demonstration of itself.
node rx "Ingress ge-0/0/1\nfilter ge-0/0/\\d+" shape=rounded
node lookup "Unicast RIB lookup" shape=box
# The escape `\"` puts a literal double quote inside a quoted label. The label
# is one token to the parser either way — the quotes delimit it, the backslash
# says "this quote is data".
node verdict "Arrived on the \"upstream\" interface?" shape=diamond
node accept "Accept and forward" shape=rounded
node drop "Drop (RPF fail)" shape=rounded class=fail

# `#` inside a bracketed edge label. Before `VERBATIM-REGION-SCOPE` this needed quoting, because
# the comment scanner ran before the label scanner and ate the rest of the line;
# the ruling made a verbatim region protect EVERY mark, not just the one that
# earned the rule. So `[VRF #7]` is four characters of label, not a comment.
edge src -[VRF #7]-> rx
edge rx -> lookup
edge lookup -[next-hop ge-0/0/1]-> verdict
edge verdict -[yes]-> accept
edge verdict -[no]-> drop class=fail

# `left` is the whole point of this document: the scene's flow axis runs from
# the source on the right back to the verdict on the left. One `flow` line per
# document (a second is a line error) — direction is a property of the figure.
flow left
rank accept,drop

# Layout zone: geometry only, no meaning. Strip it and the figure still says
# the same thing (`GUI-WRITEBACK-STRUCTURE`).
layout
# A negative coordinate. `at=` is canvas px, and the canvas origin is a
# reference point, not a wall: this nudge places the two verdict outcomes
# above the main axis, and the engine shifts the whole scene so the rendered
# canvas still begins at (0,0). Nothing is clipped and no other pin has to be
# rewritten to compensate.
pin drop at=(-30,0)
